;; mcp.edn -- external MCP servers, whose tools become rows in this session's
;; tool table.
;;
;; Copy this to ~/.clj-harness/mcp.edn (the configuration home) and edit. A
;; project can also carry its own at <project>/.harness/mcp.edn, and the two do
;; NOT compose: the project file REPLACES the whole :servers map. A project that
;; declares one server declares its own set, and a project whose file says
;; {:servers {}} declares NONE -- it does not inherit the user level's. That is
;; the same rule hooks.edn gets per point and harness.edn per key: what will
;; actually run should be readable in one file, not inferred from how two nest.
;;
;; A MISSING file is "nothing was said": no server is declared, no tool appears.
;; A file that EXISTS and cannot be read -- not EDN, not a map, a key other than
;; :servers, a name that is not usable, a declaration that does not validate --
;; is a NAMED failure carrying the absolute path.
;;
;; AN EMPTY FILE IS NOT THE EMPTY CONFIGURATION. A file of zero bytes, or one
;; holding nothing but comments, reads as `nil` and is refused by name. The way
;; to declare no servers is {:servers {}} -- which is what this file's body is.
;;
;; Every call re-reads the file: an edit takes effect at the next use of the
;; table, not at the next restart.
;;
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; ONE DECLARATION PER SERVER, AND ITS TRANSPORT IS READ OFF IT
;;
;;   :command ".."    a non-empty string: a LOCAL PROCESS, spoken to over stdio.
;;   :url ".."        a non-empty string: a REMOTE HTTP ENDPOINT.
;;
;; Exactly one of the two. Both, or neither, fails by name -- there is
;; deliberately no :type field, so a URL can never be spawned as a command and a
;; command can never be fetched as a URL.
;;
;; The rest, all optional, all validated at read time:
;;
;;   :args [...]      a vector of STRINGS, only meaningful with :command. They
;;                    are quoted as POSIX words and joined with the command into
;;                    ONE line, which the pinned login shell then runs -- so
;;                    {:command "npx" :args ["@playwright/mcp@latest"]} becomes
;;                    `npx '@playwright/mcp@latest'`. Nothing here is trusted to
;;                    be free of spaces.
;;   :env {..}        a map of STRING -> STRING, only meaningful with :command.
;;                    ADDED to the inherited environment, never replacing it: a
;;                    server declared with one token still needs PATH to find its
;;                    own runtime. These values never reach a log line and never
;;                    reach an endpoint response, at any depth -- the ledger says
;;                    a server IS configured, never with what.
;;   :timeout 60000   milliseconds, positive integer, default 60000. It bounds
;;                    ONE request -- the handshake's tools/list and every
;;                    tools/call. After a timeout the connection is DROPPED
;;                    rather than reused: a request still in flight will answer
;;                    eventually, and every message after it would be off by one.
;;
;; Any other key -- including a misspelling such as :commandd -- fails by name.
;;
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; SERVER NAMES
;;
;; A name becomes part of every tool name the server provides
;; (`mcp__<server>__<tool>`), so it must match ^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$ and must NOT
;; contain `__`. Without the second rule, server `a__b` + tool `c` and server `a`
;; + tool `b__c` produce the same tool name -- two different servers sharing one
;; name, a collision the model cannot see and no log line records.
;;
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; WHERE A stdio SERVER PROCESS STARTS
;;
;; Its working directory is the bound project's canonical directory. A session
;; bound to no project gets the directory the harness process was started in.
;; (A hook command, by contrast, is never given a working directory at all --
;; see hooks.edn.example.)
;;
;; One process per (project, server, declaration shape), shared by every session
;; of that project. Editing the declaration makes it a DIFFERENT connection, so
;; the file cannot be changed out from under a running process. A server that
;; died, or that wrote a line that is not JSON, is reconnected on the next use --
;; re-spawned, re-handshaken, its tool list asked for again. A declaration
;; DELETED from the file leaves the table as it goes and its process is reaped.
;;
;; The client speaks MCP revision 2025-06-18. A server that answers with a
;; DIFFERENT revision it supports is accepted: the version is a negotiation, not
;; a test.
;;
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; HOW TO TELL WHETHER IT WORKED
;;
;;   GET /api/mcp?threadId=..          the ledger: every declared server, its
;;                                     transport, status, reason and tool list.
;;   Settings -> MCP servers           the same thing as a panel, with a switch
;;                                     per server.
;;
;; Four states, each an observation rather than a mood:
;;
;;   connected   the handshake worked; its tools are in the table
;;   failed      the last use did not work -- WITH THE REASON
;;   disabled    this SESSION switched it off; its tools stay in the table and
;;               their calls are refused, naming the server and how to switch it
;;               back on. Its process is closed, and the roster it last reported
;;               is what stays visible.
;;   idle        declared, not yet used -- the roster is asked for on the way to
;;               the first run, so before that there is nothing to report
;;
;; failed and disabled are NOT merged in the UI: a failed server reconnects by
;; itself on the next use, a disabled one does not.
;;
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; THE BODY. This is the default state: the key, with no servers under it.
{:servers {}}

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; WORKED EXAMPLES. Each is a COMPLETE FILE BODY: replace the map above with the
;; whole thing. Everything outside the map is a comment.
;;
;; 1. A stdio server started by a command on PATH. `npx @latest` may have to
;;    DOWNLOAD the package on its first run, which is what the timeout is for: a
;;    number too small for that is the difference between "it works" and "it
;;    timed out once and looked broken".
;;
;;    {:servers {"playwright" {:command "npx"
;;                             :args ["@playwright/mcp@latest"]
;;                             :timeout 120000}}}
;;
;; 2. A local script with a token in its environment. The token is invisible in
;;    the ledger and in every audit line.
;;
;;    {:servers {"workshop" {:command "node"
;;                           :args ["/abs/path/server.js"]
;;                           :env {"SOME_TOKEN" ".."}
;;                           :timeout 60000}}}
;;
;; 3. A remote endpoint. No process, no :args, no :env.
;;
;;    {:servers {"depot" {:url "https://example.com/mcp"}}}
;;
;; 4. TWO SERVERS AT ONCE -- the normal shape of a real file. Nothing merges
;;    across the two FILES, but any number of servers sit under :servers.
;;
;;    {:servers {"playwright" {:command "npx" :args ["@playwright/mcp@latest"]}
;;               "depot"      {:url "https://example.com/mcp"}}}
;;
;; WHAT THE TOOLS ARE CALLED. `<server>` and the server's own tool names are
;; joined as `mcp__<server>__<tool>`. A bridged name longer than 64 characters,
;; or containing anything outside [a-zA-Z0-9_-], is SKIPPED BY NAME, never
;; truncated -- truncation would turn two tools into one name. The ledger lists
;; what was skipped and why. A tool's :parameters are the server's own
;; JSON Schema, unchanged.
